<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Herschel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Frederick William Herschel, Friedrich Wilhelm Herschel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Herschel was a German–British astronomer and composer, born in the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, who followed his father into the military band of Hanover and emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1757. While working as an organist and director of concerts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> he took up astronomy in 1766 and built his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescopes</a>, and on 13 March 1781 he noted an object in Gemini that was confirmed as a new planet, later named Uranus, the first discovered since antiquity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> appointed him Court Astronomer. He worked closely with his sister Caroline Herschel, published catalogues of nebulae in 1802 and 1820, and discovered infrared radiation and moons of Uranus and Saturn. He died in 1822.</p></div>
